  "infoOptionDecimals": "The decimals can be hidden, globally per value type in Settings or for this value in edit mode. Hidden decimals are cut off, not rounded.",
  "infoOptionDetailsAngle": "The detail line below the value line shows the same angle in degrees. It can be hidden for this value in edit mode.",
  "infoOptionDetailsDate": "The detail line below the value line shows the ISO week number (weeks start on Monday, week 1 contains the first Thursday) and the day of the year. It can be hidden globally or for this value.",
  "infoOptionDetailsZone": "The detail line below the value line shows the time zone abbreviation and UTC offset. It can be hidden globally or for this value. Within 7 days before a clock change, a notice about it replaces the line, and stays even if the line is hidden.",
  "infoOptionRemaining": "Can show what is left until the next midnight of its time zone instead of what has elapsed (edit mode: \"Show what is left of the day\").",
  "infoOptionSeconds": "The seconds can be hidden, globally per value type in Settings or for this value in edit mode.",
  "infoRemainingDayMinute": "Remaining: from `1440:00` at midnight down to `0:01`. On a day with a clock change it starts at `1380:00` or `1500:00` (Lord Howe: `1410:00` or `1470:00`; Troll: `1320:00` or `1560:00`).",
  "infoRemainingDayPercent": "Remaining: from `100.000` at midnight down to just above `0`. It relates to the real length of the day: on a day with a clock change that is 23 or 25 hours (Lord Howe: 23.5 or 24.5; Troll: 22 or 26), not 24.",
  "infoRemainingDaySecond": "Remaining: from `86400` at midnight down to `1`. On a day with a clock change it starts at `82800` or `90000` (Lord Howe: `84600` or `88200`; Troll: `79200` or `93600`).",
  "infoRemainingDecimalTime": "Remaining: from `10ʰ00ᵐ00ˢ` at midnight down to `0ʰ00ᵐ01ˢ`; together with the elapsed time it always adds up to 10 decimal hours.",
  "infoRemainingDoomsdayClock": "Remaining: the time to midnight as a duration, e. g. `00:01:25`.",
  "infoRemainingNewEarthTime": "Remaining: from `360°` at midnight UTC down to just above `0°`.",
  "infoRemainingSwatchBeats": "Remaining: from `1000` at midnight BMT down to just above `0`, without the `@` that marks a moment.",
  "infoRemainingTime": "Remaining: a duration without AM/PM, from `24:00:00` at midnight down to `00:00:01`; on a day with a clock change from 23 or 25 hours.",
  "infoTextAnalogClock12h": "The current time in the selected timezone as an analog clock with a 12-hour face. The hour hand turns twice a day; from noon on, \"+12\" appears below the 12.",
  "infoTextAnalogClock24h": "The current time in the selected timezone as an analog clock with a 24-hour face. The hour hand turns once a day. Minutes and seconds are read on the outer scale, hours on the inner one.",
  "infoTextBinaryClockBcd": "Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each decimal digit in binary (BCD, binary-coded decimal) in its own column of circles. Lit circles stand for 1, unlit circles for 0. The bottom circle is worth 2^0 = 1, above it 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc.; a digit is the sum of its lit circles. Tens digits need 2 or 3 bits, units digits 4.",
  "infoTextBinaryClockColumns": "Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each as one binary number in its own column (5 bits for hours, 6 for minutes and seconds). Lit circles stand for 1, unlit circles for 0. The bottom circle is worth 2^0 = 1, above it 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc.; a number is the sum of its lit circles.",
  "infoTextBinaryClockString": "Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each written as a binary number with 5, 6 and 6 digits. The rightmost digit of a number is worth 2^0 = 1, to its left 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc. Example: 10110:110000:010111 is 22:48:23.",
  "infoTextCcsdsJulianDate": "CCSDS Julian Date (CCSDS JD) = JD − 2,436,204.5 (epoch: `1958-01-01 00:00:00 UT`). Introduced by the Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems (CCSDS) for standardized spacecraft time codes, using 1958 as the historical reference date of International Atomic Time (TAI).",
  "infoTextCnesJulianDate": "CNES Julian Date (CNES JD) = JD − 2,433,282.5 (epoch: `1950-01-01 00:00:00 UT`). Introduced by the French space agency CNES as a compact day count for spaceflight calculations, using the beginning of 1950 as its reference epoch.",
  "infoTextDate": "The current calendar date in the selected timezone.",
  "infoTextDateTime": "The current date and time in the selected timezone, combined in one value. Useful e.g. for world clock tabs and copying a complete timestamp to the clipboard.",
  "infoTextDayMinute": "Minutes elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, from `0` to `1,439`. On a day with a clock change the last minute is `1,379` or `1,499` (Lord Howe: `1,409` or `1,469`; Troll: `1,319` or `1,559`).",
  "infoTextDayPercent": "Percentage of the day elapsed in the selected timezone, from `0` to just under 100. It relates to the real length of the day: on a day with a clock change that is 23 or 25 hours (Lord Howe: 23.5 or 24.5; Troll: 22 or 26), not 24.",
  "infoTextDaySecond": "Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, from `0` to `86,399`.\nOn a day with a clock change the last second is `82,799` or `89,999` (Lord Howe: `84,599` or `88,199`; Troll: `79,199` or `93,599`).",
  "infoTextDecimalTime": "Decimal time, briefly in use in France in the 1790s, divides the day into 10 decimal hours of 100 decimal minutes of 100 decimal seconds, from `0h 00m 00s` to `9h 99m 99s`. On a normal day a decimal second lasts 0.864 s; on a day with a clock change the decimal day stretches or shrinks with the real day.",
  "infoTextDoomsdayClock": "The Doomsday Clock is a symbol of how close humanity is to self-destruction, set by the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists since 1947. Epoch shows the setting as a time of day; latest: `23:58:35`, i.e. `85` seconds to midnight (January 2026). With Time Shift, historical settings are shown.",
  "infoTextDublinJulianDate": "Dublin Julian Date (DJD) = JD − 2,415,020 (epoch: `1899-12-31 12:00:00 UT`). Defined by the International Astronomical Union (IAU) in 1955 at its Dublin meeting as a convenient day count for astronomical calculations.",
  "infoTextGmst": "Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (GMST) – the hour angle of the vernal equinox at Greenwich, i.e. which part of the sky is overhead there. A sidereal day lasts 23 h 56 min 4.09 s, almost 4 minutes less than a solar day, so GMST gains about 3 min 56 s per day on the clock and runs through one more day than the clock over a year.",
  "infoTextGps": "GPS time counts seconds since `1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC` without any leap seconds. It matched UTC at the start; UTC has since added 18 leap seconds, so GPS time is 18 s ahead of UTC and 19 s behind TAI. From 2027 UTC adds no more leap seconds, so the gap stays at 18 s.",
  "infoTextHexadecimalTime": "Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, shown in base 16 (hexadecimal) and grouped in pairs from the right: from `0_00_00` to `1_51_7F` on a normal day. A day with a clock change ends earlier or later.",
  "infoTextJulianDate": "Julian Date (JD) counts days since noon on January 1, 4713 BC in the proleptic Julian calendar (epoch: `-4712-01-01 12:00:00 UT`). The standard time reference in astronomy for calculating intervals between events. A JD is the Julian day number plus the fraction of a day since the preceding noon UT; at midnight UT it therefore ends in .5.",
  "infoTextLmst": "Local Mean Sidereal Time (LMST) – the hour angle of the vernal equinox at your location: GMST plus your longitude (15° = 1 hour).",
  "infoTextModifiedJulianDate": "Modified Julian Date (MJD) = JD − 2,400,000.5 (epoch: `1858-11-17 00:00:00 UT`). Starts at midnight rather than noon, and has smaller numbers. Introduced by the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory in 1957 for satellite tracking, including the orbit of Sputnik.",
  "infoTextModifiedJulianDate2000": "Modified Julian Date 2000 (MJD2000) = JD − 2,451,544.5 or MJD − 51,544 (epoch: `2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC`). Used in geodesy, geophysics, and spaceflight to simplify calculations with smaller numbers by shifting the reference to the beginning of the year 2000.",
  "infoTextNewEarthTime": "New Earth Time (NET, 1999), also called degree time, splits the day into `360 degrees`: `0°` is midnight, `180°` noon. A degree lasts 4 minutes and is divided into 60 intervals of 4 seconds. There are no time zones: the whole world shares the same degree time. It is based on UTC+0.",
  "infoTextOctalTime": "Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, shown in base 8 (octal) with six digits: from `000000` to `250577` on a normal day. A day with a clock change ends earlier or later.",
  "infoTextReducedJulianDate": "Reduced Julian Date (RJD) = JD − 2,400,000 (epoch: `1858-11-16 12:00:00 UT`). A simple reduction of the Julian Date used mainly in astronomy to make the numbers shorter, while retaining the original noon-based start of the Julian day.",
  "infoTextRomanNumeralsTime": "The current time in the selected timezone, shown in Roman numerals. Roman numerals have no zero, so, as medieval scholars did, N for nulla (Latin for 'none') stands in for it.",
  "infoTextSevenSegmentClock": "The current time in the selected timezone in alarm clock design.",
  "infoTextSwatchBeats": "Swatch Internet Time (1998) divides the day into 1000 .beats of 86.4 seconds each. There are no time zones: the whole world shares the same .beat time. It is based on UTC+1, which Swatch calls Biel Mean Time (BMT).",
  "infoTextTai": "International Atomic Time (TAI, French: Temps Atomique International) is a continuous scale without leap seconds: atomic clocks just keep ticking, and it is UTC that gets adjusted, so TAI is 37 s ahead of UTC. Epoch shows TAI the way Unix time counts (seconds since `1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC`), i.e. Unix time plus 37. According to a decision by the General Conference on Weights and Measures in October 2026, there will be no more leap seconds, so the 37 s stay.",
  "infoTextTime": "The current time in the selected timezone.",
  "infoTextUnixSeconds": "Seconds since the Unix epoch, `1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC`. Unix time counts every day as exactly 86,400 seconds and ignores leap seconds. A signed 32-bit counter overflows on 2038-01-19 at 03:14:07 UTC (the \"Year 2038 problem\"); Time Shift stops there, too.",
